    "lawCode": "Amd §131-s, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A7449\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to extending the\nutility repayment agreements from one year to two years\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill extends the time period for mandatory repayment agreements for\napplicants for public assistance, supplemental security income (SSI)\nbenefits and additional state payments, from one year to two years, for\nhouseholds whose gross household income exceeds the applicable public\nassistance standard of need and who apply for utility arrears payments\npursuant to Social Services Law (SSL) § 131-s(1).\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend SSL § 131-s(1) to extend the time\nperiod for mandatory repayment agreements for applicants from one year\nto two years.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nSSL § 131-s(1) enables social services districts to provide utility\narrears payments on behalf of certain applicants for public assistance,\nSSI or additional state payments in order to prevent a utility shut-off\nor to restore the applicants' utility services. Such applicants whose\ngross household income exceeds the applicable public assistance standard\nof need must sign repayment agreements and repay the utility assistance\nwithin one year.\n\nPrior legislative history:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nSSL § 131-s (1) provides that social services districts may pay utility\ncompanies to avoid shut-offs or to restore service for arrearages in an\namount of up to the four most recent months in which service was\nprovided. Those households whose gross income exceeds the applicable\npublic assistance standard of need must sign repayment agreements and\ncurrently must repay the utility assistance within one year.\n\nIn the current home energy climate, utility costs are constantly in flux\nand can seriously affect the low-income population that social services\ndistricts serve. High monthly payments for repayment of utility assist-\nance can result in households falling behind in their obligations. By\nextending the period of time for low income households to repay their\n\nutility arrears assistance from one year to two years, this bill will\nreduce each household's monthly payment amounts, thus increasing their\nability to comply with repayment agreements. This bill may also improve\nsocial services districts' collections on utility repayments.\n\nLastly, by increasing the ability of low-income households to comply\nwith the terms of the repayment agreements, this bill lessens the need\nfor the social service districts to utilize costly temporary housing\noptions. Temporary housing options are utilized when applicants for SSL\n§ 131-s have been noncompliant with past repayment agreements. If there\nis noncompliance with prior repayment agreements, social services\ndistricts are prohibited from making additional payments under SSL\n131-s.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill has no fiscal impact; it would simply spread out repayment\nover two years.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill would take effect immediately upon enactment.\n\n",
